insert into public.chapters (id, story_id, number, title, body, status, published_at, created_at) values
  ('f66c51cf-1ea3-50c7-a1fd-db96cdb9769e', '05777f18-1094-593a-aa62-a5c8e1560e84', 1, 'A Very Polite Kidnapping', 'Marisol Quint had been kidnapped exactly once before, at age nine, by a goat. It had been a very determined goat and she had come home smelling of hay and triumph.

This was worse. This involved a sack, a rowing boat, and a man with an infuriatingly good voice who apologised for the sack.

''It''s only until we clear the harbour,'' he said, as if that made it better. ''The Admiral''s men are touchy about their navigators.''

''The Admiral,'' Marisol said through the sackcloth, ''is going to hang you from your own mast.''

''He''ll have to find it first,'' said Captain Dorian Vey. ''That''s rather the point of you.''

The Gull''s Lament rolled through a long, lazy swell, and the rigging sang a single high note in the wind.

"I have never once lost an argument. I have simply left several early," Dorian said.

On the horizon, a pale light blinked once where no lighthouse should be.', 'published', now() - interval '119.732 days', now() - interval '119.732 days'),
  ('2df0edc3-2750-5045-aad6-5825f7cc4549', '05777f18-1094-593a-aa62-a5c8e1560e84', 2, 'The Gull''s Lament', 'In which Marisol meets the crew, including quartermaster Bree, and steals a knife at dinner. Dorian notices and lets her keep it.

The night sky over the open water was so thick with stars it looked spilled.

Dorian Vey was a liar, a thief and a kidnapper. He was also the only person who had ever asked her opinion first.

"I have never once lost an argument." Dorian paused. "I have simply left several early."

Salt crusted the rail, and the sea ran silver under a moon that seemed in no hurry to set.

She had planned her escape four times now, and each time the plan had somehow needed him in it.

Marisol said, "Stars don''t lie, Captain. People do. That''s why I prefer the stars."

By dawn the wind had changed, and so, she suspected, had she.', 'published', now() - interval '115.852 days', now() - interval '115.852 days'),
  ('9ec1a75a-f606-5ca2-af31-01c61f92a653', '05777f18-1094-593a-aa62-a5c8e1560e84', 3, 'Escape Attempt One', 'In which Marisol slips overboard in a rowing boat at night. The tide brings her straight back alongside the ship by dawn, and Dorian hands her a cup of tea.

The Gull''s Lament rolled through a long, lazy swell, and the rigging sang a single high note in the wind.

The stars above the Lament were the same ones she had always known. She was the one who kept changing.

Dorian said, "You can hate me and still be the best navigator on this sea. Both things can be true at once."

Gulls wheeled over the wake, quarrelling over scraps the cook tossed overboard.

Dorian Vey was a liar, a thief and a kidnapper. He was also the only person who had ever asked her opinion first.

"I have never once lost an argument." Dorian paused. "I have simply left several early."

The chart in her hands showed a sea that had not been there the night before.', 'published', now() - interval '112.010 days', now() - interval '112.010 days'),
  ('a300f1cb-8d07-5c0d-a691-6adbae419787', '05777f18-1094-593a-aa62-a5c8e1560e84', 4, 'The Star Ledger', 'In which Dorian shows her his mother''s star ledger, which records constellations no Admiralty chart lists. Marisol is furious that she is fascinated.

The night sky over the open water was so thick with stars it looked spilled.

Marisol told herself the warmth in her chest was anger. It was very convincing anger.

"The captain''s smiling at charts again. That''s either very good or very bad for us," Bree said.

Salt crusted the rail, and the sea ran silver under a moon that seemed in no hurry to set.

The stars above the Lament were the same ones she had always known. She was the one who kept changing.

"You can hate me and still be the best navigator on this sea. Both things can be true at once," Dorian said.

The chart in her hands showed a sea that had not been there the night before.', 'published', now() - interval '107.866 days', now() - interval '107.866 days'),
  ('f4c7bddf-8061-5d92-a796-dc3cff7da3c2', '05777f18-1094-593a-aa62-a5c8e1560e84', 5, 'Storm Line', 'In which a sudden storm nearly wrecks the Lament. Marisol takes the wheel when the helmsman falls and steers them through by reading lightning against the clouds.

The Gull''s Lament rolled through a long, lazy swell, and the rigging sang a single high note in the wind.

Marisol had spent her life reading courses for other people. It was strange, being asked where she wanted to go.

"If you two are going to glare at each other all night, at least do it where I can''t hear it," Bree said.

Gulls wheeled over the wake, quarrelling over scraps the cook tossed overboard.

Marisol told herself the warmth in her chest was anger. It was very convincing anger.

"The captain''s smiling at charts again." Bree paused. "That''s either very good or very bad for us."

By dawn the wind had changed, and so, she suspected, had she.', 'published', now() - interval '104.232 days', now() - interval '104.232 days'),
  ('79c2f3f6-eae2-5785-a10a-f58a58ee464f', '05777f18-1094-593a-aa62-a5c8e1560e84', 6, 'A Truce, Temporary', 'In which grateful and soaked, the crew toasts Marisol. She and Dorian agree to a truce until the Drowned Light is found, and not a minute longer.

The night sky over the open water was so thick with stars it looked spilled.

