insert into public.chapters (id, story_id, number, title, body, status, published_at, created_at) values
  ('60a86d8a-7699-5087-a64e-adcb92292b54', '8543874c-59aa-5c49-a7a1-7ac4191db19f', 1, 'The House on the Shore', 'The estate agent would not come past the gate. He handed Tess the keys through the bars and said the lake was lovely this time of year, and then he drove away without looking at it.

Tess looked at it for him. Hollowmere Water was flat and black and very still, the kind of still that makes you want to throw something into it to prove it is real. The house stood on the shore with its back to the village, as if it had been told to stand in the corner.

Inside, every mirror had been turned to face the wall. Every single one, even the little round one in the bathroom cabinet. Her aunt had been a tidy woman. Someone had done this carefully.

The lake lay flat and black under a low sky, and not a single bird crossed it.

"Folk here don''t talk about the draining." Gideon paused. "Not because it''s secret. Because it''s polite."

That night, something knocked on the back door, three times, slowly, from the lake side.', 'published', now() - interval '129.718 days', now() - interval '129.718 days'),
  ('ab3d60f6-d181-5059-a08f-14bdf33aa8e1', '8543874c-59aa-5c49-a7a1-7ac4191db19f', 2, 'The Calendar', 'In which a calendar in the kitchen has one date circled: the night the village drains the lake. It is three weeks away.

The house ticked and settled around her, old timbers cooling, old pipes knocking somewhere deep in the walls.

She was starting to understand that her aunt had not been afraid of the lake. She had been afraid for it.

"Folk here don''t talk about the draining. Not because it''s secret. Because it''s polite," Gideon said.

Fog came off the water at dusk and crept up the garden as far as the kitchen step, and no further.

She had come to Hollowmere to get away from things. It was beginning to feel like the things had followed her.

"Every village keeps a promise. Ours is simply older than most," Reverend Ashby said.

In the morning there were wet footprints on the kitchen floor, leading in from the garden and stopping at the mirror.', 'published', now() - interval '125.040 days', now() - interval '125.040 days'),
  ('576e73ba-a22d-5a19-a168-dc11f297ae69', '8543874c-59aa-5c49-a7a1-7ac4191db19f', 3, 'Gideon Ward', 'In which Tess meets Gideon, the village''s young sluice-keeper, who tells her about the draining but changes the subject when she asks why.

The lake lay flat and black under a low sky, and not a single bird crossed it.

Every answer in this place arrived with a smile, and every smile seemed to close a door behind it.

Gideon said, "Whatever you hear tonight, don''t open the door to it. Not even if it sounds like someone you love."

Church bells rang across the water, slow and flat, and the sound seemed to sink rather than carry.

She was starting to understand that her aunt had not been afraid of the lake. She had been afraid for it.

Gideon said, "Folk here don''t talk about the draining. Not because it''s secret. Because it''s polite."

