  ('5eff730c-0695-56be-aa3c-cb90d985d60e', '889c7e53-53ba-5022-a566-f39b3dc8b12c', 26, 'Read Slowly', 'In which Leyla returns to the empty Exchange and translates Hasan Bey''s note one last time, and finds it ends with a line she missed: ''Thank your father for me.''

Evening light turned the bay to copper, and the steamers at anchor sent long smudges of smoke across the sky.

A ledger was a confession with the feelings taken out. She only had to put them back in.

"Silk remembers every hand that touched it." Madame Vasse paused. "People are less reliable."

Bales of silk stood stacked to the rafters of the Exchange, and the air smelled of dye, dust and lamp oil.

Hasan Bey had trusted her before they ever met. Leyla was not sure she deserved it.

"You read numbers like other people read letters from home," Aris said.

Leyla kept the red ledger on the shelf above her desk for the rest of her life. Students who came to learn translation from her sometimes asked why it was there, bound in leather and full of other people''s money.

''Because it taught me the most important thing,'' she always told them. ''Every page is written by someone hoping to be understood. Read slowly.''', 'published', now() - interval '37.769 days', now() - interval '37.769 days')
