insert into public.chapters (id, story_id, number, title, body, status, published_at, created_at) values
  ('d075727c-8725-5bbe-aaab-482459032667', '28fba78c-6ad1-5894-a2d1-78f8f994db83', 1, 'Level Zero', '[SYSTEM INTEGRATION COMPLETE. CLASS ASSIGNED: HEALER. LEVEL: 0.]

[SKILL ACQUIRED: TRANSFER (I). Move one wound from a target to yourself. Cooldown: 1 minute.]

Nadia Okoro read the blue text hanging in the air in front of her for a long time. Around her, in the queue outside the community centre, people were shouting about fireballs and sword skills and stat points.

The man behind her, a [Berserker], leaned over her shoulder and laughed. ''Level zero? I didn''t know that was allowed.''

''Neither did I,'' said Nadia, and closed the window, and started thinking about what a person could do with other people''s wounds.

The dungeon gate shimmered in the middle of the car park, blue and humming, with a queue of adventurers checking their gear in its light.

"Most healers stand at the back. You keep standing at the front. Why is that?" Theo said.

[NOTICE: AN UNUSUAL GROWTH PATTERN HAS BEEN DETECTED.]', 'published', now() - interval '87.892 days', now() - interval '87.892 days'),
  ('607cf831-6752-5421-a3dd-21d6a2165aed', '28fba78c-6ad1-5894-a2d1-78f8f994db83', 2, 'The Guild Queue', 'In which every party at the new Adventurers'' Guild rejects Nadia. A recruiter suggests she work in a field clinic instead.

Somewhere ahead in the dark, something with too many legs skittered across the stone and was gone.

Nadia wondered, not for the first time, whether the System had made a mistake, or a very particular bet.

"Most healers stand at the back. You keep standing at the front. Why is that?" Theo said.

Stone corridors stretched ahead, lit by veins of glowing crystal that pulsed slowly like a sleeping heartbeat.

Every point of pain she took was a point of experience. The System was very fair, and completely heartless.

Her status window flickered, and for a moment her class name was written in a language she could not read.', 'published', now() - interval '85.362 days', now() - interval '85.362 days'),
  ('dcca8fcf-a234-5248-a66a-d61fab78c204', '28fba78c-6ad1-5894-a2d1-78f8f994db83', 3, 'Field Clinic', 'In which working at the clinic, Nadia learns that taking wounds onto herself earns experience. She levels up for the first time after a long, painful night.

The dungeon gate shimmered in the middle of the car park, blue and humming, with a queue of adventurers checking their gear in its light.

She had been told no a hundred times since the integration. She had stopped hearing it.

"You take everyone''s pain and walk it off like it''s weather." Theo paused. "It''s terrifying to watch."

Rain drummed on the corrugated roof of the field clinic, and the beds were never empty for long.

Nadia wondered, not for the first time, whether the System had made a mistake, or a very particular bet.

"Most healers stand at the back. You keep standing at the front. Why is that?" Theo said.

Her status window flickered, and for a moment her class name was written in a language she could not read.', 'published', now() - interval '83.104 days', now() - interval '83.104 days'),
  ('4d5a3b29-d314-57c8-ad86-c83abcee53db', '28fba78c-6ad1-5894-a2d1-78f8f994db83', 4, 'Solo', 'In which Nadia enters the beginner dungeon alone. She heals herself by moving her wounds onto defeated monsters, a trick no guide mentions.

Somewhere ahead in the dark, something with too many legs skittered across the stone and was gone.

Nadia had learned to count wounds the way other people counted coins: how many she could carry, how far.

"Your class is a joke, Okoro." Rafi paused. "Your stubbornness, though, that''s a rare skill."

Stone corridors stretched ahead, lit by veins of glowing crystal that pulsed slowly like a sleeping heartbeat.

She had been told no a hundred times since the integration. She had stopped hearing it.

"You take everyone''s pain and walk it off like it''s weather." Theo paused. "It''s terrifying to watch."

Behind her, deep in the dungeon, a door she had not seen before quietly swung open.', 'published', now() - interval '80.792 days', now() - interval '80.792 days'),
