insert into public.chapters (id, story_id, number, title, body, status, published_at, created_at) values
  ('bd6ddad1-d88d-5173-a545-126ee169a71f', 'beb87e81-0de9-5bb9-afdf-d6a5634c0f32', 1, 'The Dented Bell', 'The bell over the door of Lindqvist Books rang like it had been waiting for her. It was a small brass bell with a dent in it, and Hana remembered reaching up on tiptoe to make it ring, aged six, while her grandmother pretended to be startled every single time.

The shop smelled the same. Paper, dust, the faint salt of the harbour, and something like cardamom that had soaked into the shelves over fifty years of buns eaten behind the counter.

Hana put down her suitcase and stood in the middle of it all and waited to feel something. What she felt, mostly, was tired. She had been tired for so long she had forgotten it was not a permanent condition.

Rain tapped on the bookshop window, and the harbour beyond was a soft grey smudge of masts and gulls.

"Fifty years at sea, and the best thing I ever caught was a decent sonnet," Elof said.

The next morning, there was a loaf of bread on the step again, still warm, with a note she could not read.', 'published', now() - interval '104.737 days', now() - interval '104.737 days'),
  ('33cc4e87-902c-5f17-a927-66ea0453d78b', 'beb87e81-0de9-5bb9-afdf-d6a5634c0f32', 2, 'Elof', 'In which a retired fisherman named Elof comes in every morning to read poetry in the armchair and never buys anything. Hana tries to sell him a book and fails.

Down on the quay, the fishing boats came in one by one, and the whole town seemed to set its clocks by them.

Hana noticed she had stopped checking her phone in the mornings. She noticed, too, that nobody had died of it.

"Fifty years at sea, and the best thing I ever caught was a decent sonnet," Elof said.

The stove in the back room ticked as it warmed, and the kettle began its slow climb toward a whistle.

She kept a list on the counter titled THINGS TO FIX BEFORE SELLING. It was getting shorter, and somehow she was getting sadder.

Mirela said, "Your grandmother never once paid for bread. She paid in books. I''m keeping the tradition."

An estate agent called, asking if she was still planning to sell. Hana said yes, and did not sound sure.', 'published', now() - interval '100.556 days', now() - interval '100.556 days'),
  ('c4e8b858-8fb0-5206-ac30-760ff480f35e', 'beb87e81-0de9-5bb9-afdf-d6a5634c0f32', 3, 'Bread on the Step', 'In which loaves of bread start appearing on the shop step each dawn. Hana tracks the culprit to Mirela, the baker, who says the deal was made with her grandmother.

Rain tapped on the bookshop window, and the harbour beyond was a soft grey smudge of masts and gulls.

Her grandmother had never written down how to run a bookshop. Hana was beginning to see that it was all written in the regulars.

"Nobody comes here to buy books, lass. They come because it''s warm and you listen," Elof said.

Snow fell over the harbour so quietly that the town seemed to hold its breath to listen.

Hana noticed she had stopped checking her phone in the mornings. She noticed, too, that nobody had died of it.

That evening the bell over the door rang, though nobody came in.', 'published', now() - interval '96.351 days', now() - interval '96.351 days'),
  ('ab67b570-b604-5fd4-a544-1415c0b0348a', 'beb87e81-0de9-5bb9-afdf-d6a5634c0f32', 4, 'THINGS TO FIX', 'In which Hana writes a list of repairs needed before selling. Elof fixes the creaking stair without being asked and refuses payment.

Down on the quay, the fishing boats came in one by one, and the whole town seemed to set its clocks by them.

Hana had spent ten years answering emails at midnight. Here, the loudest thing at midnight was the sea.

"Can I just sit here for a while?" Saga paused. "I''ll buy something. Eventually. Probably."

The stove in the back room ticked as it warmed, and the kettle began its slow climb toward a whistle.

Her grandmother had never written down how to run a bookshop. Hana was beginning to see that it was all written in the regulars.

"Nobody comes here to buy books, lass." Elof paused. "They come because it''s warm and you listen."

Out on the quay, the first boat of the season was coming home, and the whole town went down to meet it.', 'published', now() - interval '92.306 days', now() - interval '92.306 days'),
  ('18ace619-c73d-5735-af59-78aa8800c6f9', 'beb87e81-0de9-5bb9-afdf-d6a5634c0f32', 5, 'Saga', 'In which a quiet teenager named Saga starts hiding in the poetry corner after school. Hana leaves her alone and puts out a second cushion.

Rain tapped on the bookshop window, and the harbour beyond was a soft grey smudge of masts and gulls.

