insert into public.chapters (id, story_id, number, title, body, status, published_at, created_at) values
  ('0f8d60c4-6e94-5965-a874-a5cdf314a138', '0d34a01c-205a-5dc5-aed1-7231e016c33a', 1, 'Release', 'Inés Ortega walked out of Valdera Women''s Prison at 7:04 on a Tuesday morning carrying a plastic bag, a coat that no longer fitted, and five years of reasons not to forgive him.

Marco was waiting in a borrowed car across the road. He had rehearsed what to say for a month. When she got in and closed the door and looked at him, he forgot all of it.

''You look old,'' she said.

''You look free.''

''Not yet.'' She took a folded map out of the plastic bag and spread it on the dashboard. It was a construction plan for the new Red Line metro, and somebody had circled a point beneath the old cathedral square in pen. ''Nine days,'' she said. ''Then I''ll be free.''

The city glittered below the rooftop, its traffic a slow red river on the ring road.

"You plan for everything except people, Marco. That''s always been your problem," Inés said.

When Marco returned to the safe house, someone had moved the map on the table by exactly one inch.', 'published', now() - interval '139.729 days', now() - interval '139.729 days'),
  ('80d444d0-c53e-5f4d-af4e-478441805780', '0d34a01c-205a-5dc5-aed1-7231e016c33a', 2, 'The Vault', 'In which the vault belongs to Councillor Ferro, the man who framed Inés. It holds the ledgers proving he bribed his way into the metro contract.

The safe house was a flat above a closed laundrette, and it still smelled faintly of soap and old steam.

The city had always felt like a lock to Marco. Lately it had started to feel like a home.

"You plan for everything except people, Marco." Inés paused. "That''s always been your problem."

Construction lights burned all night over the new metro tunnels, and the whole square hummed with machinery.

He kept counting days: nine, eight, seven. Time was the only alarm you couldn''t disarm.

Inés said nothing all the way back. That was never a good sign.', 'published', now() - interval '135.270 days', now() - interval '135.270 days'),
  ('7187e092-1cf3-5e3f-a9a1-46866b1e6f2b', '0d34a01c-205a-5dc5-aed1-7231e016c33a', 3, 'Nine Days', 'In which the Red Line opens in nine days, sealing the construction tunnels forever. Marco agrees, and starts building a crew.

The city glittered below the rooftop, its traffic a slow red river on the ring road.

Inés was better at this than she had been five years ago. Prison had taught her to plan, too.

"I didn''t come out to get even." Inés paused. "I came out to get my name back."

Down in the tunnels the air was cold and gritty, and every footstep came back twice off the curved walls.

The city had always felt like a lock to Marco. Lately it had started to feel like a home.

"You plan for everything except people, Marco." Inés paused. "That''s always been your problem."

Somewhere beneath the cathedral square, a vault door logged an access that should not have happened.', 'published', now() - interval '130.921 days', now() - interval '130.921 days'),
