Down in the tunnels the air was cold and gritty, and every footstep came back twice off the curved walls.

Every plan Marco drew had the same flaw at its centre: him, and the job that went wrong.

"Every security system has a person behind it. People are the easiest lock," Dash said.

When Marco returned to the safe house, someone had moved the map on the table by exactly one inch.', 'published', now() - interval '52.423 days', now() - interval '52.423 days'),
  ('c528f0af-8f39-5b6a-a1ab-cbc0596214cd', '0d34a01c-205a-5dc5-aed1-7231e016c33a', 22, 'Copies', 'In which Dash scans the ledgers and sends copies to every newspaper, prosecutor and council member in the city before Ferro can react.

The safe house was a flat above a closed laundrette, and it still smelled faintly of soap and old steam.

The city had always felt like a lock to Marco. Lately it had started to feel like a home.

Inés said, "You plan for everything except people, Marco. That''s always been your problem."

Construction lights burned all night over the new metro tunnels, and the whole square hummed with machinery.

He kept counting days: nine, eight, seven. Time was the only alarm you couldn''t disarm.

Inés said nothing all the way back. That was never a good sign.', 'published', now() - interval '47.875 days', now() - interval '47.875 days'),
  ('dcdff6ba-7cf4-58cf-a110-a802cf5e3acf', '0d34a01c-205a-5dc5-aed1-7231e016c33a', 23, 'Opening Day', 'In which on the metro''s opening day, Ferro cuts the ribbon as police arrive to arrest him on the platform, live on television.

The city glittered below the rooftop, its traffic a slow red river on the ring road.

Inés was better at this than she had been five years ago. Prison had taught her to plan, too.

"I didn''t come out to get even. I came out to get my name back," Inés said.

Down in the tunnels the air was cold and gritty, and every footstep came back twice off the curved walls.

The city had always felt like a lock to Marco. Lately it had started to feel like a home.

On the evening news, Councillor Ferro announced the Red Line would open two days early.', 'published', now() - interval '43.554 days', now() - interval '43.554 days'),
  ('41201cee-e409-5500-a064-659d12f52d70', '0d34a01c-205a-5dc5-aed1-7231e016c33a', 24, 'Retrial', 'In which with Ferro''s ledgers as evidence, Inés''s conviction is overturned. She walks out of the courthouse with her name back.

The safe house was a flat above a closed laundrette, and it still smelled faintly of soap and old steam.

Marco had planned forty jobs. This was the first one where he cared more about the people than the payout.

"I can get us in. Getting us out is a separate invoice," Dash said.

Construction lights burned all night over the new metro tunnels, and the whole square hummed with machinery.

Inés was better at this than she had been five years ago. Prison had taught her to plan, too.

When Marco returned to the safe house, someone had moved the map on the table by exactly one inch.', 'published', now() - interval '39.062 days', now() - interval '39.062 days'),
  ('ec261db8-86c5-5565-a177-aa27a2e7dec2', '0d34a01c-205a-5dc5-aed1-7231e016c33a', 25, 'Red Line', 'In which months later, the crew rides the Red Line together to Benita''s new bakery opening. Inés finally tells Marco she forgives him.

The city glittered below the rooftop, its traffic a slow red river on the ring road.

Every plan Marco drew had the same flaw at its centre: him, and the job that went wrong.

"Every security system has a person behind it. People are the easiest lock," Dash said.

Down in the tunnels the air was cold and gritty, and every footstep came back twice off the curved walls.

Marco had planned forty jobs. This was the first one where he cared more about the people than the payout.

"I can get us in." Dash paused. "Getting us out is a separate invoice."

The bakery was called One Metre. Benita said it was a joke about the rock, and the sign-writer said it was a strange name for a bakery, and the crew said nothing at all and ordered coffee.

On the wall behind the counter hung a framed metro ticket, the first ever sold on the Red Line, bought by a woman who had waited five years to ride it and had chosen, in the end, to ride it with friends.', 'published', now() - interval '34.918 days', now() - interval '34.918 days')
