insert into public.chapters (id, story_id, number, title, body, status, published_at, created_at) values
  ('f0ec9238-b437-56ae-a874-eb5f858190cf', 'd7c9dac0-6e19-521b-ae48-f40d07b58268', 1, 'Skerrow', 'The ferry to Skerrow ran twice a week in summer and whenever the skipper felt like it in winter. It was winter. Nell Carrick waited three days.

When she finally stepped onto the island''s single stone jetty, the lighthouse was the only thing taller than the grass. It stood on the headland like a white finger raised for silence.

The keeper''s cottage had been locked since 1987. The key was stiff, the hinges stiffer, and inside the air was cold and perfectly still. A table set for one. A plate, a fork laid across it, a mug with a brown ring dried at the bottom. On the chair, a coat, folded as if its owner had only stepped out to check the lamp.

Wind came off the Atlantic in long grey breaths, flattening the grass and rattling the cottage windows in their frames.

"Everyone on Skerrow has a theory about Angus Reid. Most of them are about the sea," Morag said.

The lighthouse lamp, unlit for years, was burning when she looked out of the window at midnight.', 'published', now() - interval '124.846 days', now() - interval '124.846 days'),
  ('a1c96bec-7d4f-5c47-abd6-30d91ee56da9', 'd7c9dac0-6e19-521b-ae48-f40d07b58268', 2, 'The Logbook', 'In which Nell reads keeper Angus Reid''s logbook. Its final entry is dated the night after the storm in which he supposedly drowned.

Peat smoke drifted from the island''s few chimneys and hung low over the rooftops in the damp air.

Every answer on Skerrow came wrapped in a kindness, and every kindness asked her to stop looking.

"Everyone on Skerrow has a theory about Angus Reid. Most of them are about the sea," Morag said.

The lighthouse beam swept slowly over the island, touching each stone and wall and fence post once and moving on.

The logbook''s handwriting was patient and exact. It was not the hand of a man about to throw himself off a cliff.

Out on the rocks below the headland, the tide had uncovered something that glinted.', 'published', now() - interval '119.958 days', now() - interval '119.958 days'),
  ('fffd8c02-8aeb-58ea-ab83-cfa8d7c19c92', 'd7c9dac0-6e19-521b-ae48-f40d07b58268', 3, 'Morag''s Shop', 'In which at the island''s only shop, Morag tells Nell the official story: Angus checked the lamp during the storm and was swept off the cliff.

Wind came off the Atlantic in long grey breaths, flattening the grass and rattling the cottage windows in their frames.

On an island this small, Nell realised, everyone was a witness. The question was what they had agreed to forget.

"Islands keep secrets the way rock keeps water." Morag paused. "Slowly, and all the way down."

The tide drew back across the bay, leaving long ribs of sand and the dark backs of rocks that were usually hidden.

Every answer on Skerrow came wrapped in a kindness, and every kindness asked her to stop looking.

Morag stopped speaking to her in the shop the next morning, and so did everyone else.', 'published', now() - interval '115.525 days', now() - interval '115.525 days'),
  ('fcaf2518-a90e-5a44-a8a8-dfa909dbdb27', 'd7c9dac0-6e19-521b-ae48-f40d07b58268', 4, 'Callum', 'In which Nell meets Callum, a fisherman whose grandfather led the search that night. He offers to help, mostly because no one else will talk to her.

Peat smoke drifted from the island''s few chimneys and hung low over the rooftops in the damp air.

Nell had catalogued the belongings of a hundred dead strangers. None of them had felt quite this unfinished.

"My grandfather rowed out looking for him that night." Callum paused. "He never talked about what he found."

The lighthouse beam swept slowly over the island, touching each stone and wall and fence post once and moving on.

On an island this small, Nell realised, everyone was a witness. The question was what they had agreed to forget.

Morag said, "Islands keep secrets the way rock keeps water. Slowly, and all the way down."

When the ferry came, it brought a letter for Nell with no stamp and no return address.', 'published', now() - interval '110.866 days', now() - interval '110.866 days'),
  ('26535b4c-ee57-5132-a52d-b44cdc9605c1', 'd7c9dac0-6e19-521b-ae48-f40d07b58268', 5, 'Sergeant Baird', 'In which The retired sergeant who closed the case visits Nell and asks her, politely, to catalogue the furniture and leave the logbook alone.

Wind came off the Atlantic in long grey breaths, flattening the grass and rattling the cottage windows in their frames.

She found herself setting the cottage table each evening the way Angus had left it, as though he might come back to finish.

