  ('d31f1d32-660e-54b7-a294-3ec2141bb4c5', 'd7c9dac0-6e19-521b-ae48-f40d07b58268', 21, 'Catalogue Complete', 'In which Nell finishes her catalogue. The lighthouse is bought not by a developer but by the island itself, pooling thirty-five years of guilt and savings.

Wind came off the Atlantic in long grey breaths, flattening the grass and rattling the cottage windows in their frames.

The logbook''s handwriting was patient and exact. It was not the hand of a man about to throw himself off a cliff.

"It was a storm, a cliff and a lonely man." Sergeant Baird paused. "The case is closed, miss, and it ought to stay that way."

The tide drew back across the bay, leaving long ribs of sand and the dark backs of rocks that were usually hidden.

She found herself setting the cottage table each evening the way Angus had left it, as though he might come back to finish.

Callum said, "You read old paper like it might bite you. Maybe you''re right to."

Nell''s catalogue of Skerrow Light ran to two hundred and twelve items. The last entry, number 212, read simply: ''One table, set for one, with plate, fork and mug. Left as found, at the request of the island.''

She never told anyone that on her last evening she had washed the mug and set it back on the table, clean, the way you do when someone is finally coming home.', 'published', now() - interval '30.701 days', now() - interval '30.701 days')
