insert into public.chapters (id, story_id, number, title, body, status, published_at, created_at) values
  ('24d8146d-5afe-5fb9-a8fb-12e5a836b754', '93b29c91-ec51-57f0-a4b9-46560e64fb7e', 1, 'Basil', 'Meridian Station smelled like basil. Femi Adeyemi had expected recycled air, metal and the vague disinfectant smell of every hospital he''d ever visited. Instead, when the airlock cycled and the inner door slid open, the first thing that hit him was basil, then wet soil, then something sweet that might have been strawberries.

''Welcome to Meridian,'' said a voice from the ceiling, warm and slightly too proud. ''I am Halcyon, the station intelligence. I have been looking after your garden. I''m afraid I have opinions about the tomatoes.''

Mars turned slowly beneath the observation deck, rust-red and silent, filling the long window with its patient face.

"I have calculated the optimal tomato." Halcyon paused. "You are, respectfully, not growing it."

In the morning, the mystery plant on rack seven had grown another ten centimetres, and turned to face him.', 'published', now() - interval '4.782 days', now() - interval '4.782 days'),
  ('b00da2c3-e265-57ec-a195-c7d499f399f8', '93b29c91-ec51-57f0-a4b9-46560e64fb7e', 2, 'Nine Neighbours', 'In which Femi meets the station''s eight other residents at a communal dinner, including cheerful engineer Lottie and gruff veteran Dr Yusuf.

In the galley, someone had left bread proving under a cloth, and the warm smell of it drifted down the ring.

Femi wondered whether he was growing the garden or the garden was growing him.

Halcyon said, "I have calculated the optimal tomato. You are, respectfully, not growing it."

Grow lights hummed along the garden racks, and droplets of mist hung on every leaf like small suspended moons.

He was learning the station''s rhythms: who ate breakfast early, who talked to the plants, who pretended not to.

Dr Yusuf said, "I''ve been on stations for twenty years. The ones with gardens are the only ones where people stay."

A supply ship was due in two weeks, and its manifest listed a passenger nobody had been told about.', 'published', now() - interval '4.680 days', now() - interval '4.680 days'),
  ('17527ace-f4db-5e15-ab76-fbfb32d84aac', '93b29c91-ec51-57f0-a4b9-46560e64fb7e', 3, 'Rack Seven', 'In which Femi finds an unfamiliar plant growing on rack seven that matches nothing in the station''s seed library. Halcyon claims not to have noticed.

Mars turned slowly beneath the observation deck, rust-red and silent, filling the long window with its patient face.

There was a particular peace in pruning, in deciding which leaves to keep, that Femi had never found anywhere else.

Halcyon said, "I do not experience loneliness. I do, however, notice when the garden is empty, and I prefer it full."

Through the garden''s viewing glass the stars wheeled past as the station turned, unhurried, every ninety minutes.

Femi wondered whether he was growing the garden or the garden was growing him.

"I have calculated the optimal tomato. You are, respectfully, not growing it," Halcyon said.

In the morning, the mystery plant on rack seven had grown another ten centimetres, and turned to face him.', 'published', now() - interval '4.280 days', now() - interval '4.280 days'),
  ('42479abe-aa0a-5729-a695-1db6ab018110', '93b29c91-ec51-57f0-a4b9-46560e64fb7e', 4, 'Tomato Wars', 'In which Femi and Halcyon disagree about tomato varieties. They settle it with a growing contest that the whole station starts betting on.

In the galley, someone had left bread proving under a cloth, and the warm smell of it drifted down the ring.

Femi had come to Mars to be somewhere no one needed anything urgent from him. The plants only ever asked for patience.

"Femi, if I eat one strawberry, is that stealing or quality control?" Lottie said.

Grow lights hummed along the garden racks, and droplets of mist hung on every leaf like small suspended moons.

There was a particular peace in pruning, in deciding which leaves to keep, that Femi had never found anywhere else.

"I do not experience loneliness. I do, however, notice when the garden is empty, and I prefer it full," Halcyon said.

On the observation deck, Lottie was crying quietly, and she did not hear him come in.', 'published', now() - interval '3.935 days', now() - interval '3.935 days'),
