insert into public.chapters (id, story_id, number, title, body, status, published_at, created_at) values
  ('0bd7134b-2b8c-5c49-a534-c83a11220a78', '12ccfce4-55c7-5715-a619-c32aae79e154', 1, 'Again', 'Old Brannoc taught sword in the back room of a dockside tavern, in exchange for drink and the occasional fish. He taught Kira Thane because she was the only one who kept coming back after the first lesson, which involved being knocked into a barrel.

''Again,'' he said, every time. Not unkindly. Not kindly either. Just ''again'', as though it were the only word for swordplay that mattered.

Kira had been hearing it for six years. Tonight, as the ninth moon of the ninth year rose over the harbour, Brannoc did not say it. He handed her his old sword, wrapped in sailcloth, and said, ''Go and sign your name.''

The harbour smelled of tar, fish and rain, and the masts stood in the fog like a forest of bare trees.

"Again." Old Brannoc paused. "The sword doesn''t care how tired you are. Neither does the person holding the other one."

When she returned to the inn, someone had left a black ribbon tied around the hilt of her sword.', 'published', now() - interval '79.854 days', now() - interval '79.854 days'),
  ('a2f9eb57-7555-58ef-af0e-f4409cf19ed7', '12ccfce4-55c7-5715-a619-c32aae79e154', 2, 'Nine Cities', 'In which at the opening ceremony Kira sees champions from all nine Free Cities, including Selene Ardent, the undefeated duellist who disgraced Brannoc twenty years ago.

The ninth moon hung low and swollen over the Free Cities, silver on every rooftop and blade.

Kira was beginning to understand her teacher had not been drinking to forget. He had been drinking to remember less.

Old Brannoc said, "Again. The sword doesn''t care how tired you are. Neither does the person holding the other one."

Lanterns hung over the tournament grounds, and the crowd''s noise rose and fell like a sea against a wall.

She had never fought anyone who had been trained since childhood. She was learning fast what that meant.

A messenger from the mines brought a letter in Tobin''s hand, shorter than the last.', 'published', now() - interval '77.210 days', now() - interval '77.210 days'),
  ('c4717319-d91a-5636-ae08-9f0515c760f2', '12ccfce4-55c7-5715-a619-c32aae79e154', 3, 'First Bout', 'In which Kira faces a noble''s son in the first round and wins by fighting like a dockworker: low, dirty and very fast.

The harbour smelled of tar, fish and rain, and the masts stood in the fog like a forest of bare trees.

Her hands still smelled of rope and salt. She hoped it would remind her opponents where she came from, and frighten them.

"Every fight is two questions: where are they going to be, and where are you going to be instead?" Old Brannoc said quietly.

Steel rang somewhere in the practice yards, a steady rhythm of strike and parry that went on long past midnight.

Kira was beginning to understand her teacher had not been drinking to forget. He had been drinking to remember less.

"Again. The sword doesn''t care how tired you are. Neither does the person holding the other one," Old Brannoc said.

A messenger from the mines brought a letter in Tobin''s hand, shorter than the last.', 'published', now() - interval '74.446 days', now() - interval '74.446 days'),
  ('b19a526a-39bd-503d-a80a-f5868ad298e9', '12ccfce4-55c7-5715-a619-c32aae79e154', 4, 'The Crowd', 'In which the crowd adopts Kira as the ''harbour blade''. Fishermen start betting their week''s wages on her.

The ninth moon hung low and swollen over the Free Cities, silver on every rooftop and blade.

Kira counted her breaths between bouts the way Brannoc taught her, slow and low, like hauling rope.

"Win, Kira." Tobin paused. "Then come and get me. I''ll wait. I''m good at waiting now."

Lanterns hung over the tournament grounds, and the crowd''s noise rose and fell like a sea against a wall.

Her hands still smelled of rope and salt. She hoped it would remind her opponents where she came from, and frighten them.

