insert into public.chapters (id, story_id, number, title, body, status, published_at, created_at) values
  ('77a4e744-1a09-532c-a87d-398ccaf0eac9', 'fe5a7c01-daca-566f-a1ab-0017b479c792', 1, 'Facing the Window', 'The first bird Aiko ever folded flew out of her hands and straight into the kitchen fire. She was six. She burned her fingers getting it out, and her grandmother, without looking up from the rice, said: ''Next time, fold it facing the window.''

That was the whole of the lesson. That was the whole of the secret.

Nine years later, Aiko still folded her birds facing the window, at night, by the light of the river. She sent them out over the rooftops to watch the town for her and come back with what they saw, and she never, ever told anyone what she could do.

Mist rose off the river at dawn and climbed the terraced fields until the whole valley seemed to float.

"Paper is patient." Grandmother Setsu paused. "It remembers the tree it came from. Ask it nicely."

At the window, a paper bird she had not folded was waiting, its wings wet from the river.', 'published', now() - interval '49.949 days', now() - interval '49.949 days'),
  ('8fee4071-3d6e-5db6-a4d8-0e26a3a4f29f', 'fe5a7c01-daca-566f-a1ab-0017b479c792', 2, 'The Shrine''s Rule', 'In which Aiko learns why: the last girl with the gift, her grandmother''s sister, was taken by the Shrine to serve the river spirits and never came home.

In Grandmother Setsu''s house the floor mats smelled of straw, and the kettle sang softly over the hearth.

Somewhere between her hands and the sky, the paper became something else. She had never been able to see the moment it happened.

"Paper is patient. It remembers the tree it came from. Ask it nicely," Grandmother Setsu said.

Paper lanterns swayed along the shrine path, and their light trembled on the wet stone steps.

Every fold was a small question to the paper. Aiko had learned to wait for the answer.

Ren was standing in her doorway in the morning, holding one of her birds, and he was not smiling.', 'published', now() - interval '48.089 days', now() - interval '48.089 days'),
  ('23d573be-3bac-5a51-a4e9-9bc776b680dc', 'fe5a7c01-daca-566f-a1ab-0017b479c792', 3, 'Ren', 'In which Aiko''s friend Ren, the bridge-builder''s son, catches one of her birds in flight and works out her secret immediately.

Mist rose off the river at dawn and climbed the terraced fields until the whole valley seemed to float.

The town loved its river and feared it in equal measure. Aiko was starting to think the river felt the same way about the town.

"The Shrine is not cruel, child. It is afraid. Fear makes people tidy," Grandmother Setsu said.

Cedar trees crowded the mountainside, their tops lost in cloud, their roots knotted over stones older than the town.

Somewhere between her hands and the sky, the paper became something else. She had never been able to see the moment it happened.

Grandmother Setsu said, "Paper is patient. It remembers the tree it came from. Ask it nicely."

Ren was standing in her doorway in the morning, holding one of her birds, and he was not smiling.', 'published', now() - interval '46.451 days', now() - interval '46.451 days'),
  ('90dcb3f4-a7f2-5b85-addc-df36856c3a1e', 'fe5a7c01-daca-566f-a1ab-0017b479c792', 4, 'The Bridges', 'In which The town holds its spring festival asking the River Lord''s permission to repair the bridges. For the first time anyone remembers, the river does not answer.

In Grandmother Setsu''s house the floor mats smelled of straw, and the kettle sang softly over the hearth.

Aiko had spent her life hiding the one thing she was best at. She was tired, suddenly, of hiding.

"You''re terrible at keeping secrets, Aiko. You smile at birds," Ren said.

Paper lanterns swayed along the shrine path, and their light trembled on the wet stone steps.

The town loved its river and feared it in equal measure. Aiko was starting to think the river felt the same way about the town.

Grandmother Setsu said, "The Shrine is not cruel, child. It is afraid. Fear makes people tidy."

At the window, a paper bird she had not folded was waiting, its wings wet from the river.', 'published', now() - interval '44.522 days', now() - interval '44.522 days'),
  ('062a673d-5470-5e15-ac9a-5a0a0b19ac90', 'fe5a7c01-daca-566f-a1ab-0017b479c792', 5, 'Silence', 'In which The priests are troubled by the river''s silence. Aiko sends a paper bird over the water at night, and it comes back soaked and trembling.

Mist rose off the river at dawn and climbed the terraced fields until the whole valley seemed to float.

Her grandmother''s silences were not empty. Aiko was learning to read them like creases in paper.

"If you can fold a bird that flies, can you fold one that comes back with good news?" Ren said quietly.

Cedar trees crowded the mountainside, their tops lost in cloud, their roots knotted over stones older than the town.

Aiko had spent her life hiding the one thing she was best at. She was tired, suddenly, of hiding.

The river had risen a hand''s width overnight, and the oldest bridge was groaning.', 'published', now() - interval '43.051 days', now() - interval '43.051 days'),
